## Feature 1: User Authentication, Security & JWT Session Management

### 1.1 Overview & Architecture
GalleryHub employs a stateless, token-based authentication mechanism utilizing JSON Web Tokens (JWT) coupled with salted bcrypt cryptographic password hashing. This subsystem provides isolated tenancy: all media assets, folder hierarchies, activity records, and sharing permissions are strictly scoped to the authenticated user ID extracted from the verified token payload.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor Client as Web Browser (React SPA)
    participant AuthAPI as /api/users/login
    participant UserModel as Mongoose User Model
    participant DB as MongoDB Atlas
    participant Mid as authMiddleware (protect)
    participant ProtectedRoute as Protected API Routes

    Note over Client,AuthAPI: Authentication Phase
    Client->>AuthAPI: POST { email, password }
    AuthAPI->>UserModel: findOne({ email: email.toLowerCase().trim() }).select('+password')
    UserModel->>DB: Execute query
    DB-->>UserModel: User document with password hash
    UserModel-->>AuthAPI: User object
    AuthAPI->>AuthAPI: bcrypt.compare(password, user.password)
    alt Invalid Credentials
        AuthAPI-->>Client: 401 Unauthorized { message: 'Invalid email or password' }
    else Valid Credentials
        AuthAPI->>AuthAPI: jwt.sign({ id: user._id }, JWT_SECRET, { expiresIn: '30d' })
        AuthAPI-->>Client: 200 OK { _id, name, email, profilePicture, token }
        Client->>Client: localStorage.setItem('userInfo', JSON.stringify(data))
    end

    Note over Client,ProtectedRoute: Authorization Phase (Subsequent Requests)
    Client->>Mid: GET /api/images (Header: Bearer <token>)
    Mid->>Mid: jwt.verify(token, JWT_SECRET)
    alt Verification Fails (Expired / Tampered)
        Mid-->>Client: 401 Unauthorized { message: 'Not authorized, token failed' }
    else Verification Succeeds
        Mid->>UserModel: findById(decoded.id).select('-password')
        UserModel->>DB: Execute query
        DB-->>UserModel: User entity
        UserModel-->>Mid: User document
        Mid->>Mid: Attach req.user = user; req.user.id = user._id
        Mid->>ProtectedRoute: next()
        ProtectedRoute-->>Client: 200 OK (User-Scoped Data)
    end
```

### 1.2 Deep Technical Implementation

#### Registration Pipeline
When a prospective user signs up, the request lands on `POST /api/users/register`. The incoming payload contains `name`, `email`, and `password`. The controller normalizes the email address by applying `.toLowerCase().trim()` to prevent duplicate accounts arising from casing inconsistencies. It evaluates whether an account already exists with `User.findOne({ email: normalizedEmail })`. If an existing record matches, it terminates early with a `400 Bad Request` status code. 

If unique, a new record is created using `User.create({ name, email: normalizedEmail, password })`. Here, Mongoose lifecycle middleware intercepts the transaction via a `pre('save')` hook configured in `backend/models/User.js`. The hook checks `if (!this.isModified('password')) return next();`. When true, it invokes `bcrypt.genSalt(10)` to compute a cryptographic salt, followed by `bcrypt.hash(this.password, salt)` to generate an irreversible digest. The raw plaintext password is never written to disk or database logs. Upon successful creation, `generateToken(user._id)` issues a 30-day signed JWT containing `{ id: user._id }`, and the API responds with a `201 Created` payload containing the user profile and session token.

#### Login & Verification Engine
Authentication occurs through `POST /api/users/login`. Because the Mongoose schema sets `select: false` on the password attribute to prevent accidental exposure in general queries, the authentication query explicitly chains `.select('+password')`. Once retrieved, the schema instance method `user.matchPassword(enteredPassword)` executes `await bcrypt.compare(enteredPassword, this.password)`. This method performs constant-time string comparison to defend against timing attacks. If verification succeeds, a freshly generated JWT is dispatched alongside the user details.

#### The `authMiddleware.js` Verification Guard
All private endpoints route through the `protect` middleware function. The middleware inspects the incoming HTTP request headers for an `Authorization` header beginning with the string `'Bearer '`. It splits the header string by space delimiter to isolate the raw JWT string. Using `jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET)`, it decodes and cryptographically verifies the token signature against the server's private secret key. Upon signature validation, it fetches the user record via `User.findById(decoded.id).select('-password')`. If the user has been deleted from the database since token issuance, it returns `401 Unauthorized`. Otherwise, it injects the hydrated Mongoose document into `req.user`, establishes `req.user.id = req.user._id` for API backwards compatibility, and invokes `next()` to proceed to the controller.

#### Client-Side State Synchronization
In the React application, session state is orchestrated in `frontend/src/App.js`. Upon initial DOM mounting, a `useEffect` reads the serialized session string from `localStorage.getItem('userInfo')`. If present, it initializes the `userInfo` React state. The JWT token string is passed down the component tree to sub-pages and modal dialogues. To ensure authenticated access, `react-router-dom` v6 defines a protected layout: navigating to `/gallery` when `token` evaluates to `null` triggers an immediate `<Navigate to="/" replace />` redirection to the landing page. Conversely, visiting the root `/` while authenticated redirects automatically to `/gallery`. When a user clicks "Sign Out", `handleLogout()` calls `localStorage.removeItem('userInfo')` and sets `setUserInfo(null)`, instantly tearing down all user-scoped views and memory references.

