## Feature 2: Multi-Format Media Upload & Processing Engine

### 2.1 Overview & Architecture
GalleryHub incorporates a unified ingestion engine capable of handling high-volume uploads across three distinct media formats: raster and vector images, compressed video streams, and digital workplace documents. The pipeline orchestrates client-side file inspection, drag-and-drop event capturing, multi-part form-data streaming via Multer, asynchronous video transcoding/thumbnailing via FFmpeg, and contextual folder routing.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["User drags/selects files in UploadModal"] --> B["Client File Classifier (classifyFile)"]
    B --> C{"Check 50MB Size Limit"}
    C -- Exceeds --> D["Toast Error: Exceeds 50MB"]
    C -- Passes --> E["Generate Local Blob Thumbnails (createObjectURL)"]
    E --> F["Render Preview Cards in Modal"]
    
    F --> G["User Clicks 'Upload'"]
    G --> H["Group Files by MediaType (image, video, document)"]
    
    subgraph Parallel Upload Dispatch
        H --> I1["Images -> POST /api/images/upload-multiple"]
        H --> I2["Videos -> POST /api/videos/upload-multiple"]
        H --> I3["Documents -> POST /api/documents/upload-multiple"]
    end
    
    subgraph Multer Processing
        I1 --> J1["Multer diskStorage -> backend/uploads/"]
        I2 --> J2["Multer diskStorage -> backend/videos/"]
        I3 --> J3["Multer diskStorage -> backend/documents/"]
    end
    
    J1 --> K1["Image.insertMany() & logActivity()"]
    J2 --> K2["Video.insertMany() & logActivity()"]
    J3 --> K3["Document.insertMany() & logActivity()"]
    
    K2 --> L["Asynchronous setImmediate Background Task"]
    subgraph FFmpeg Processing Engine
        L --> M1["ffmpeg.screenshots(): Capture Frame at 1.0s -> 320x240 JPG"]
        L --> M2["ffmpeg.ffprobe(): Probe Video Stream for Exact Duration"]
        M1 & M2 --> M3["Video.findByIdAndUpdate(): Save thumbnail & duration"]
    end
    
    K1 & M3 & K3 --> N{"Is folderId present?"}
    N -- Yes --> O["PUT /api/folders/:id/move (Assign fileIds to Folder)"]
    N -- No --> P["Return 201 Created to Frontend"]
    O --> P
    P --> Q["Toast Success & Invalidate Gallery State Cache"]
```

### 2.2 Deep Technical Implementation

#### Client-Side Inspection & Classification
When files are dropped into the `UploadModal` dropzone or picked via `<input type="file">`, the component invokes `classifyFile(file)`:
```javascript
const classifyFile = (file) => {
  if (file.type.startsWith('image/')) return 'image';
  if (file.type.startsWith('video/')) return 'video';
  return 'document';
};
```
Every individual file is evaluated against a strict 50 MB threshold (`50 * 1024 * 1024` bytes). Oversized files are rejected immediately with descriptive `react-hot-toast` alerts. For allowed files, the component distinguishes between image previews (instantiated via `URL.createObjectURL(file)`) and non-image files, assigning contextual iconography:
- 📕 for `.pdf`
- 📘 for `.doc`, `.docx`
- 📊 for `.xls`, `.xlsx`, `.csv`
- 📑 for `.ppt`, `.pptx`
- 📦 for `.zip`, `.rar`, `.7z`
- 🎥 for video files

#### Multer Storage Architecture
On the Express server, Multer disk storage configurations are defined separately across `imageRoutes.js`, `videoRoutes.js`, and `documentRoutes.js`. Storage destinations are resolved using `path.join(__dirname, '..', '<folder>')`. Filename collision prevention is achieved by prefixing original filenames with millisecond-precision timestamps: `Date.now() + '-' + file.originalname`. File filters enforce MIME integrity:
- **Images**: RegEx pattern `/jpeg|jpg|png|gif|webp/` matching both `file.mimetype` and `path.extname`.
- **Videos**: RegEx pattern `/mp4|mov|avi|mkv/`.
- **Documents**: Broad ingestion supporting all document, archive, and text extensions.

#### Parallel Multipart Transmission & Folder Injection
When the user triggers `handleUpload`, the files are grouped into three independent arrays: `imageFiles`, `videoFiles`, and `docFiles`. The client constructs separate `FormData` payloads and dispatches parallel HTTP POST requests via `Promise.all([ ...uploads ])`. If the user is currently browsing within a folder context, the modal captures `folderId`. Once the multi-part uploads resolve and return the newly generated database IDs (`uploadedImageIds`, `uploadedVideoIds`, `uploadedDocIds`), the upload handler automatically issues a follow-up batch assignment request to `PUT /api/folders/${folderId}/move`, cleanly nesting the new assets without requiring manual drag-and-drop.

#### Asynchronous Video Frame Extraction & Metadata Probing
Unlike static images, video files require visual thumbnail representations and length metadata. In `backend/routes/videoRoutes.js`, once Multer writes the video stream to disk and `Video.insertMany()` saves the records, the API dispatches the HTTP 201 response immediately to prevent frontend timeouts. It then invokes `setImmediate()` to schedule a non-blocking background worker that utilizes `fluent-ffmpeg`:
1. It executes `ffmpeg(videoPath).screenshots({ count: 1, folder: thumbnailDir, filename: thumbnailName, size: '320x240', timemarks: ['1'] })` to capture a sharp frame at the 1-second mark.
2. It calls `ffmpeg.ffprobe(videoPath)` to parse stream headers and extract container duration in seconds.
3. It performs a selective database patch: `Video.findByIdAndUpdate(video._id, { thumbnail: thumbnailName, duration })`.

