## Feature 9: Trash, Two-Stage Soft-Delete & Permanent Erasure Engine

### 9.1 Overview & Architecture
GalleryHub employs a desktop-style, two-stage file deletion architecture. Rather than destroying assets immediately upon clicking delete, the application flags files as soft-deleted, moves them into a dedicated Recycle Bin (Trash), unlinks them from active folders, and provides a 30-day recovery grace period before permanent erasure.

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> ActiveState: File Uploaded
    
    state ActiveState {
        ActiveGallery: Displayed in Gallery
        ActiveFolder: Assigned to Folder
    }
    
    ActiveState --> TrashedState: DELETE /api/:type/:id
    
    state TrashedState {
        ExcludedFromGallery: isDeleted = true
        UnlinkedFromFolder: folder = null
        TimestampMarked: deletedAt = new Date()
        RetentionCountdown: 30 - elapsedDays
    }
    
    TrashedState --> ActiveState: PUT /api/:type/:id/restore (Restore File)
    TrashedState --> ErasedState: DELETE /api/:type/:id/permanent (Permanent Delete)
    
    state ErasedState {
        UnlinkDisk: fs.unlinkSync(file.path)
        DeleteMongo: file.deleteOne()
        AuditPermanent: logActivity(permanent: true)
    }
    
    ErasedState --> [*]
```

### 9.2 Deep Technical Implementation

#### The Soft-Delete Mutation
When a user deletes a file from the gallery or preview modal, the request routes to `DELETE /api/images/:id` (or `/api/videos/:id`, `/api/documents/:id`). The controller executes soft-deletion:
```javascript
image.isDeleted = true;
image.deletedAt = new Date();
image.folder = null;
await image.save();
await logActivity(req.user.id, 'delete', image.originalName, 'image');
```
The file's binary data remains safe on disk, and its record remains in the database. Clearing `folder = null` ensures that deleting a file immediately removes it from any folder groupings.

#### Transparent Query Filtering
All primary retrieval endpoints (`GET /api/images`, `GET /api/videos`, `GET /api/documents`) automatically filter out deleted files by appending `{ isDeleted: { $ne: true } }` to their queries. This guarantees that soft-deleted items vanish from the gallery, folder views, search results, and category filters.

#### The Trash Interface & 30-Day Auto-Purge Calculation
Selecting the Trash tab on the dashboard invokes `fetchTrash()`, querying `/api/images/trash`, `/api/videos/trash`, and `/api/documents/trash`. The results are merged and sorted by `deletedAt` descending. Each trash card calculates its remaining retention window:
```javascript
const daysLeft = Math.max(0, 30 - Math.floor((Date.now() - new Date(item.deletedAt)) / 86400000));
```
The item displays a format icon, the original filename, and a tag showing how many days remain before automated expiration.

#### Restoration Mechanics
Clicking "Restore" sends a request to `PUT /api/:type/:id/restore`. The controller resets `item.isDeleted = false` and `item.deletedAt = null`, logs a `'restore'` action in the activity feed, and returns the entity. The item immediately reappears in the user's gallery in its original unfiled state.

#### Permanent Erasure Pipeline
When a user clicks the permanent delete button in the Trash tab, the UI displays a confirmation alert. Confirming sends a request to `DELETE /api/:type/:id/permanent`:
1. It validates user ownership: `file.user.toString() === req.user.id`.
2. It inspects the filesystem: `if (fs.existsSync(file.path)) fs.unlinkSync(file.path)`. This immediately reclaims physical disk storage.
3. It removes the database document: `await file.deleteOne()`.
4. It logs the permanent deletion to the audit trail with metadata `{ permanent: true }`.

