## Feature 11: Real-Time Activity Feed & Audit Logging System

### 11.1 Overview & Architecture
GalleryHub includes an audit trail system that records significant user operations, providing accountability and operational transparency. Activities are displayed in a real-time sidebar feed directly on the dashboard.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    subgraph Operation Handlers
        A1["Image / Video / Document Upload"] --> B["logActivity()"]
        A2["Soft Delete / Restore / Permanent Delete"] --> B
        A3["Folder Create / Delete / Move"] --> B
        A4["File Share Creation"] --> B
        A5["Image Editor Export"] --> B
    end

    subgraph Logging Engine
        B --> C["Activity.create({ user, action, targetName, targetType, metadata })"]
        C --> D[("Activities Collection in MongoDB")]
    end

    subgraph Presentation Feed
        E["ActivityFeed Component"] --> F["GET /api/activity?page=1&limit=20"]
        F --> D
        D --> G["Paginated Activity JSON Response"]
        G --> H["Render Sidebar Timeline with Contextual Icons & Relative Timestamps"]
    end
```

### 11.2 Deep Technical Implementation

#### The Activity Schema & Indexing
The Activity model in `backend/models/Activity.js` stores:
- `user`: Foreign key pointing to User.
- `action`: Strict enumeration: `'upload'`, `'delete'`, `'restore'`, `'move'`, `'create_folder'`, `'delete_folder'`, `'share'`, `'edit_image'`.
- `targetName`: Human-readable name of the file or folder.
- `targetType`: Enumeration: `'image'`, `'video'`, `'document'`, `'folder'`.
- `metadata`: Arbitrary mixed object for additional context (e.g., `{ permanent: true }`).
- `createdAt`: Timestamp defaulting to `Date.now`.

To ensure pagination queries execute in sub-millisecond time even as the collection grows, a compound index is defined on `{ user: 1, createdAt: -1 }`.

#### Non-Blocking Logging Helper
Activity logging is decoupled from the main HTTP response flow via a dedicated helper in `backend/routes/activityRoutes.js`:
```javascript
const logActivity = async (userId, action, targetName, targetType, metadata = {}) => {
  try {
    await Activity.create({ user: userId, action, targetName, targetType, metadata });
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Failed to log activity:', error);
  }
};
```
Because the helper wraps creation inside its own try-catch block without re-throwing errors, a logging failure will never cause an upload, delete, or share operation to fail.

#### Paginated Feed API (`GET /api/activity`)
The activity route supports pagination via `page` and `limit` query parameters:
```javascript
const limit = parseInt(req.query.limit) || 20;
const page = parseInt(req.query.page) || 1;
const skip = (page - 1) * limit;

const activities = await Activity.find({ user: req.user.id })
  .sort({ createdAt: -1 })
  .skip(skip)
  .limit(limit);

const total = await Activity.countDocuments({ user: req.user.id });
```
The endpoint returns `{ activities, total, page, pages: Math.ceil(total / limit) }`.

#### Frontend Activity Feed Component
The `ActivityFeed.js` component renders as a sticky sidebar alongside the main gallery. It maps each incoming activity record to an intuitive icon and color badge:
- 📤 Upload (Blue)
- 🗑️ Delete (Red)
- ♻️ Restore (Green)
- 📁 Folder operations (Purple)
- 🔗 Share (Yellow)
- 🎨 Image edits (Pink)

Relative timestamps are computed dynamically (e.g., "just now", "5 minutes ago", "2 hours ago", "yesterday"), giving users a clear timeline of recent actions.

